# Correction Q1: No, the render is incomplete and shows a head tilt instead of the specified pan, and it completely omits the arm movements, failing to represent the "reach" part of the cue.
# Correction Q2: The entire sequence should be changed to first establish hesitation with a clear head aversion (looking away and down), and then execute a single, slow, incomplete forward reach that ends in a hold, signifying the uncertain finality of the half-acceptance.

# Q1: How would a human perform this motion? (One sentence)
A person would avert their gaze down and to the side, then slowly extend their hand forward only part of the way, pausing in that uncertain, incomplete position.
# Q2: Pose Candidates Comparison. Rank at least 2 pose configurations (torso_height, arm_position, gripper_orientation, head, left_arm, x/y/z) and state the winner and why—do NOT pick presets from gesture names alone.
1. `torso: "low", arm_position: "down", head: "down"`. This is very withdrawn and submissive, but the subsequent reach motion would be a large "up-and-forward" movement.
2. `torso: "mid", arm_position: "back", head: "center", gripper_orientation: "vertical"`. This starts neutral but with the hand close to the body, creating a perfect setup for a simple, readable forward extension.
Winner: #2. It provides a clearer, more direct path for the "reach" part of the gesture while the "back" position still implies reservation.
# Q3: Structure Comparison. Compare 2+ full primitive sequences (e.g., M1: pose > movement > pose vs M2: pose > path > path). State the winner and why—**prioritize movement/path beats that express the cue**, not pose-only tweaks.
1. M1: `pose` -> `path (short reach)` -> `movement (look away)` -> `path (short reach)`. This intersperses the action with hesitation, which could be less clear.
2. M2: `pose` -> `movement (look away)` -> `path (single slow reach)`. This first establishes the emotional state (hesitation) with the head, then performs the physical action that reflects it.
Winner: M2. It tells a cleaner story by setting up the emotion before the action, making the subsequent slow reach clearly motivated by hesitation.
# Q4: Context Reinforcement. Compare 2+ micro-timing choices (speed, repetition, hold_time, torso/head expression) that make the gesture read stronger and pick the best one.
1. Use a very slow reach speed (e.g., 0.4) to convey hesitation.
2. Use a moderately slow speed (e.g., 0.6) but add a long `hold_time` (e.g., 1.0s) at the end of the reach. This shows the robot committing to the half-reach but then freezing, uncertain of what to do next.
Winner: #2. The long hold at the end powerfully communicates the "half-reach" concept, showing the robot has stopped and is waiting uncertainly, which is a stronger expression of hesitation than just moving slowly.
